WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.499
Six Moves, from EL Publishing. Move one: five words first. Three minutes.

2
00:00:05.949 --> 00:00:10.857
Here's the problem. A newcomer sits in your biology class. You start teaching photosynthesis.

3
00:00:10.857 --> 00:00:19.090
By the second sentence they're lost — not because they can't do science, but because four of the words in that sentence are new, and nobody said them first.

4
00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.219
The usual fix is a vocabulary list. Thirty words on Monday, a quiz on Friday.

5
00:00:24.219 --> 00:00:29.505
The English learner learns none of them, because thirty words with no context is noise.

6
00:00:29.955 --> 00:00:34.848
The move is smaller than that. Before the lesson, pick five words. Not twenty. Five.

7
00:00:34.848 --> 00:00:39.275
The ones that, if a student doesn't have them, the whole lesson falls apart.

8
00:00:39.725 --> 00:00:42.225
For photosynthesis, that's probably sunlight.

9
00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:43.370
Energy.

10
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.013
Carbon dioxide.

11
00:00:45.463 --> 00:00:46.229
Oxygen.

12
00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:47.547
And glucose.

13
00:00:47.997 --> 00:00:52.914
Put them on the board before you start. A picture or a symbol next to each one. Say each word.

14
00:00:52.914 --> 00:00:58.251
Have the whole class say it back. That's three minutes, and it's the best three minutes of the period.

15
00:00:58.701 --> 00:01:03.476
If your English learners speak Spanish, look at those five again. Four of them are cognates.

16
00:01:03.476 --> 00:01:06.901
Write the Spanish next to the English. You don't have to speak it.

17
00:01:06.901 --> 00:01:11.313
You just have to show the student that the big scary word is a word they already own.

18
00:01:11.763 --> 00:01:16.764
Then, in the lesson, use the five words on purpose. Say the word and point to it on the board.

19
00:01:16.764 --> 00:01:24.637
Use the same word every time — if you said carbon dioxide at the start, don't switch to C O two ten minutes later without saying those are the same.

20
00:01:24.637 --> 00:01:28.202
Have students say the word to a partner. Put it on the exit ticket.

21
00:01:28.652 --> 00:01:29.825
That's the whole move.

22
00:01:29.825 --> 00:01:34.409
Five words, a picture each, said out loud before the lesson, used ten times during it.

23
00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:43.578
Every level of English learner can get in the door of a lesson that starts this way — and so can the kid in the back who was never going to read the vocabulary list anyway.

24
00:01:44.028 --> 00:01:48.914
Tomorrow: pick your five. Write them on the board before the bell. That's it.

25
00:01:48.914 --> 00:01:55.069
Six Moves is free, with the rest of the library, at radan55 dot github dot io slash everyteacher.
